what's up guys and welcome to a video  that I'm really damn happy to make a  video on the Dark Souls 2 interview on  the 11th of April I think it's safe to  say that everyone in the Dark Souls  community was worried for the sequel all  it took was one little quote just one  little quote that went like this we want  to make the game more accessible and  immediately people started visualizing  this and that was all it took for many  in the community to give up completely  the amount of overreaction was  ridiculous but I like to think that  people overreacted because they feel so  passionately about the game they didn't  want to see their favorite franchise  ruined and this is why I'm happy to make  this video if you don't end up watching  any further at least know this  everything we've seen in the gameplay  reveal everything we've heard from the  developers this April looks and sounds  [ __ ] amazing so accessibility this  damn word has been the biggest point of  debate over the last few months in  December one of Dark Souls twos lead  developers said that he wanted the game  to be more accessible big mistake  immediately everyone panicked many  people even the biggest content creators  interpreted this as a step backwards for  the Soul series interpreting it as a  movement away from the hardcore nature  of the game in my video covering that  interview I expressed concern over the  word but I ended up giving the developer  the benefit of the doubt based on some  of the other things he said  so I'll just explain what I mean by that  easy games are accessible games by  definition but accessible games aren't  limited to being easy accessible just  means that they are easier to get into  and the soul series is guilty of being a  game that hides features from its  players we even like these features I'm  still learning new things about Dark  Souls years later however when core  mechanics like humanity summoning world  tendency and covenants aren't explained  well in game you have a problem when you  have to go to a wiki to learn about  these things properly you have a problem  it breaks immersion to have to look  these things up the soul series should  have simple controls and gameplay on the  surface and depth and nuance for people  who  more experienced at the game the same  goes for the law I don't mind having a  more obvious story on the surface as  long as the game retains its depth by  making the core concepts difficult to  understand you run the risk of  alienating a player who would otherwise  love the game if he just took two  minutes to look up what those things  meant and it just so happens that this  is exactly what the developers meant  when they originally said let's make the  game more accessible all they wanted to  do was make the core gameplay clearer so  that everyone can play along just long  enough to experience the deeper gameplay  concepts here's some quotes from the  interview that confirmed this players  will be able to take their experience  from Dark Souls 1 into Dark Souls 2 core  gameplay remains the same the combat  system in Dark Souls 2 will remain the  same based around simple control and  trial and error while Dark Souls has  simple controls there's a huge element  of depth to those controls and while  there is trial and error  there are usually clues and ways to  prove yourself so you don't just rely on  trial and error when you come out with a  sequel you try to add on new features  from software doesn't want to add on new  features for new players they want to  emphasize the core underlying concepts  so that those concepts can be  effectively expressed to the new players  this is so new players can also  experience what they're trying to  communicate so not necessarily  accessible but simply make it more clear  in terms of what we want to express with  the underlying concepts within Dark  Souls so hopefully those comments put to  rest your fears regarding the  accessibility of the game as I stated in  my December video the people are from  software know what lies at the core of a  Souls game here's a few more snippets  that really drive that point home around  five minutes into the 12 minute gameplay  reveal the enemies showcase different  reactions and thought processes based on  the actions of the player there's an  emphasis on the player having to  strategize to proceed and in the 11th  minute the director goes on to state  that death is still a core part of Dark  Souls in fact he wants to enhance that  giving a huge variety of deaths to  experience and here's a final quote from  the IGN interview regarding the law  there were fears that the story would be  dumbed down and easy to understand but  it appears that this will not be the  case two minutes into the Q&amp;A video the  director states that spiritual elements  to Dark Souls and the Soul series in  general is the way we story tell  we want and encourage players to look  into the key details of the game and  imagine the game for themselves create  your own story as you play through the  game and interpret to yourself we will  continue to provide this feature and  that my friends is good news for my  channel and in closing around five  minutes into the Q&amp;A the director states  that he thinks there's two underlying  concepts to the soul series one there's  a sense of satisfaction from overcoming  difficulty this is an overlying concept  we want to maintain and two there is a  loose connection with other players  throughout the world this is going to  carry over into Dark Souls 2 now this is  all great news I'm sure you agree that  the developers are trying to enhance the  core of the game which in my opinion is  the best way to go about making a sequel  no one likes drastic difference in a  sequel and if you enhance the essence of  what makes a game unique then everyone  wins in an interview with Miyazaki for  games but he confirmed that Dark Souls 2  will protect the core experience overall  I was pretty impressed with the  interview as well I'm glad that the  interviewer 5gn clearly knew what he was  talking about and he asked all the right  questions however I should mention that  I Gian weren't the only people to get  exclusive interviews the following  information is less known but no less  important most of my face for Dark Souls  2 before this interview came from Namco  Bandai Namco Bandai are the publishers  of the game and they were rumored to be  behind the decision to change the game's  directors now naturally people assumed  that this meant they wanted to change  the direction of the game as well the  publisher developer relationship is an  interesting one and it's worrying when  the publisher has more power because  they may try to push money making  decisions on the developer without  proper understanding of how that would  impact the game however I even had this  fear put to rest when I read this  interview by now gamer comm the article  reads as follows we feel that the  relationship between from software and  Namco  is good Turner Mira told us Namco Bandai  gives us lots of freedom in terms of  what we want to create and we add from  software are really able to pursue what  we want to deliver to the fans so the  relationship there is great the last  point I bring up has more to do with the  PC version of the game I play primarily  on PC and this news is particularly  heartening in an interview with shock  news you Eaton amira admitted that the  PC port of Dark Souls was not very well  done and sort of half-assed this time  around there will be more priority on PC  this time PC will even be considered and  developed from the very beginning now  Dark Souls PC was originally a port from  the console versions of the game however  this time it's actually going to be the  other way around in a translated  interview from game called calm the  developers state that the PC version  serves as a base from which it will be  adapted to consoles obviously they'll  still be developed on console separately  but it's interesting to know that this  is the case Dark Souls 2 will run at 60  frames per second on PC and a guaranteed  stable 30 frames per second on console  Dark Souls 2 won't be a next-gen console  title but it is using an entirely new  engine developed from the ground up from  A to Z in closing here's a picture of  the box up with something truly glorious  there is no games for Windows Live logo  insight this isn't a hundred percent  confirmed but it's looking pretty likely  sounds like it's time to upgrade your PC  stay tuned for more Dark Souls analysis  and next up is the gameplay  you

